class TaskRunner implements Runnable {
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (running && !Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
            processOneUnit();
        }
    }

    void stop() {
        running = false;
    }
}
```

A volatile write publishes the new flag value under the Java Memory Model. It does not promise instant scheduling or wake a thread blocked in I/O; interruption or a resource-specific cancellation action may also be needed.

```java
// WRONG: volatile guarantees visibility, never atomicity
public class Counter {
    private volatile int count = 0;
    public void increment() { count++; } // read-modify-write races -> lost updates
}
```

```java
// RIGHT: synchronized makes the compound update atomic and visible
public class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    public synchronized void increment() { count++; } // one thread at a time
    public synchronized int get() { return count; }
}
```

Use volatile for a single-writer flag; use synchronized (or AtomicInteger) when a value is read-modified-written.

```java
// WRONG: many writers + volatile counter = lost increments
public class Hits {
    private volatile int hits = 0;
    public void record() { hits++; } // not atomic
}
```

```java
class Metrics {
    private final AtomicInteger hits = new AtomicInteger();
    private volatile boolean enabled = true;

    void record() {
        if (enabled) {
            hits.incrementAndGet();
        }
    }

    int total() { return hits.get(); }
    void disable() { enabled = false; }
}
```

Each counter update is atomic. The check and increment together are **not** one atomic transaction with `enabled`; if the requirement says no increment may occur after disable, the design needs a stronger shared protocol.

